<div class="<%= layout_framework_classes %>">
  <div class="<%= layout_framework_title_classes %>">
    <%= render 'framework/page_header', page_name: :chart %>
  </div>

  <div class="<%= layout_framework_content_classes %>">
    <%= render_framework_intro_paragraph(:chart) %>

    <div class="space-y-16">
      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Usage') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Any CDN-enabled JavaScript library may be used to develop charting interfaces,
            however the examples below leverage <%= link_to 'Highcharts', 'https://highcharts.com', target: :_blank, class: 'underline' %>
            and <%= link_to 'Chartkick', 'https://chartkick.com', target: :_blank, class: 'underline' %>.
          </p>

          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Charts used to carry their own fixed colors, ignoring bit depth, dark mode, and themes
            while the rest of the screen adapted. The plugin runtime now bundles a small
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLCharts</code> helper that reads the right
            paint from the screen as the chart builds. The methods below are the ones these examples use;
            <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_charts) %> carries the full list.
          </p>

          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">series(i, n, { el })</code>: the fill for
              series <em>i</em> of <em>n</em>, correct for the current device, mode, and theme.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">applySwatches({ el })</code>: paints legend
              markers tagged <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">data-chart-series="i"</code>
              with matching series colors.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">textStyle(role, { el })</code>: resolves a
              framework typography role for SVG text.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">options({ el })</code> and
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">merge()</code>: the recommended adaptive
              Highcharts defaults, merged under your overrides.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">watch(el, buildFn)</code>: rebuilds the chart
              when device, scale, mode, dark mode or theme changes.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">paint(token, { el })</code>: a single token,
              a flat color on solid panels or a dither pattern on 1- and 2-bit screens.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">grid({ el, dir })</code> and
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">axisLine({ el })</code>: the grid-line and
              axis/tick options <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">options()</code> already applies,
              for an axis you build by hand.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">{ el }</code> is the chart container id or
            element. Omit it on a single-screen plugin.
          </p>

          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLCharts</code> is the Highcharts adapter
            built on <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint</code>, the framework's
            public JS paint API. Grid lines and plotted text use the same border and typography systems as the
            rest of the screen. For anything beyond Highcharts, use TRMNLPaint directly: see
            <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_api) %>.
          </p>

          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Highcharts numeric dimensions do not read CSS automatically. Resolve heights, spacing, line widths, and label offsets with <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint.px()</code> inside <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLCharts.watch()</code>. The watcher rebuilds the chart after a scale change, and the paint API supplies the new numbers.
          </p>

          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            If you set the <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">height: null</code> within your highchart's settings, the chart will automatically expand to fill the available space.
          </p>

          <div class="<%= message_warning_classes %>">
            <div class="flex items-center w-full">
              <div class="flex-shrink-0 items-center">
                <%= render 'shared/icons/warning' %>
              </div>
              <div class="w-full flex items-center justify-between ml-3">
                <p class="leading-none text-sm font-medium">
                  Take care to disable animation effects, otherwise your chart may be only partially captured by TRMNL's screenshot rendering service.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="<%= message_sage_classes %>">
            <div class="flex items-center w-full">
              <div class="flex-shrink-0 items-center">
                <%= render 'shared/icons/info' %>
              </div>
              <div class="w-full flex items-center justify-between ml-3">
                <p class="text-sm font-medium">
                  These examples load Highcharts and Chartkick from trmnl.com, so they render empty without
                  network. Highcharts is a commercial library that TRMNL licenses, and a custom stack brings
                  its own charting library and license.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="space-y-2">
            <%= framework_heading(4, 'Line Chart') %>
            <p class="<%= framework_subsection_description_classes %>">
